{#-
  The one #SBATCH header, included by all four job templates (AC-3.1).

  It exists so "every template emits -A, -p, -q, --gpus-per-node, --mem and
  --time" is a structural property of the templates rather than something four
  copies happen to agree on this week.  The AC-3.1 test is parametrised over
  all four templates, but a shared partial is what makes it hard to *break*.

  Gilbreth's model, and why this differs from the pre-port header:
    -A  the allocation the job is billed to.  Required on every submission.
    -p  the GPU TYPE.  Required.  This is why no generic-resource GPU request
        appears here: the GPU type is chosen by the partition, so asking for
        an A100 generic resource next to `-p a10` is a contradiction sbatch
        resolves in whichever way the user did not intend.  The documented
        request form is --gpus-per-node.
    -q  the QOS (normal / standby / training).  A QOS, never an account.
  The job-class annotation the pre-port header carried is gone entirely -- it
  named the previous cluster's job classes and means nothing here.

  Every value below comes from a rcac_cli.gilbreth.SubmissionPlan, so nothing
  reaches this file without having gone through build_directives().  The two
  exceptions are `job_name`, composed per-template in Python (AC-3.18), and
  `cpus_per_gpu`, which no documented Gilbreth rule constrains.

  Per-template directives deliberately NOT shared: `--array=` (job_array),
  `--dependency=` (pipeline) and `-o`/`-e`, whose filename patterns genuinely
  differ (%x_%A_%a for an array, %x_%j otherwise).
-#}
#SBATCH -J {{ job_name }}
#SBATCH -A {{ account }}
#SBATCH -p {{ partition }}
#SBATCH -q {{ qos }}
#SBATCH --nodes={{ nodes }}
#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node={{ ntasks_per_node }}
#SBATCH --gpus-per-node={{ gpus_per_node }}
#SBATCH --cpus-per-gpu={{ cpus_per_gpu }}
#SBATCH --mem={{ mem }}
#SBATCH --time={{ time }}
{%- if constraint %}
#SBATCH --constraint={{ constraint }}
{%- endif %}{#- the trailing newline is stripped here so each including template
    controls its own spacing; every includer supplies the newline itself -#}
